blocks.explanation:
Joint: a place where two bones, or bone and cartilage, come together. The three major kinds are fibrous (immovable), cartilaginous (slightly movable) and synovial (freely movable) joints.

Fibrous joints: the bones are directly connected by fibrous connective tissue consisting mainly of collagen. They permit no movement. Examples:
• sutures, found only between the immovable bones of the skull;
• joints between the tibia and fibula in the lower leg;
• joints between teeth and their sockets in the jawbone.

Cartilaginous joints: the bones are connected by a layer of cartilage and allow little movement. Two main types:
• bones connected by hyaline cartilage, e.g. the joint between the first rib and sternum;
• bones connected by fibrocartilage, e.g. the pubic symphysis in the pelvic girdle and the intervertebral discs.

(i) Disc slip: the intervertebral discs act as shock absorbers and help in movement. A herniated or slipped disc occurs when the outer layer of a disc tears or ruptures and the inner gel-like substance leaks out and presses against nearby nerves or the spinal cord.
Causes: trauma, degenerative changes due to aging, or repetitive strain on the vertebral column.
Symptoms: pain, numbness and tingling in the affected area, weakness or loss of muscle function, and in severe cases bowel or bladder dysfunction.

(ii) Osteoporosis: a condition of weakened bones more prone to fractures and breaks; bone density decreases, making the bones fragile and porous.
Causes:
• natural decrease of bone mass with age, more pronounced in some people;
• in women, a drop in oestrogen after menopause accelerates bone loss;
• lack of calcium and vitamin D in the diet (calcium gives strength; vitamin D helps absorb calcium);
• lack of weight-bearing exercise;
• treatments such as long-term use of corticosteroids;
• smoking and alcohol consumption.
